window.BSH_RULES = { title: "Bloodsoup Hell Rules 0.3", html: "<nav class=\"rtoc\" id=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Table of contents\"><h2>Contents</h2><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-introduction\">Introduction</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-the-coriander-system\">The Coriander System</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-starting-the-game\">Starting the Game</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-items-and-magic\">Items and Magic</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-character-death-and-reincarnation\">Character Death and Reincarnation</a><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-spiritual-guidance\">Spiritual Guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-reincarnation\">Reincarnation</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-totem-and-legacy-tokens\">Totem and Legacy Tokens</a></li></ul></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-abilities-table\">Abilities Table</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-fighting-styles-table\">Fighting Styles Table</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-weapons-table\">Weapons Table</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-weapon-types\">Weapon Types</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-interacting-with-the-world\">Interacting With the World</a><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-skill-checks-and-assists\">Skill Checks and Assists</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-passive-skills\">Passive Skills</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-critical-success-and-critical-failure\">Critical Success and Critical Failure</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-explode-tokens\">Explode Tokens</a></li></ul></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-travel-rest-and-sustenance\">Travel, Rest, and Sustenance</a><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-long-distance-travel-and-rest\">Long Distance Travel and Rest</a></li></ul></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-encounters\">Encounters</a><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-turn-based-time\">Turn-Based Time</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-action-points\">Action Points</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-stamina-use-and-recovery\">Stamina Use and Recovery</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-readied-actions\">Readied Actions</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-attacking-and-defending\">Attacking and Defending</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-health-points-and-damage\">Health Points and Damage</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-standard-encounter-actions\">Standard Encounter Actions</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-combo-attacks\">Combo Attacks</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-combo-check-table\">Combo Check Table</a></li></ul></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-conditions-afflictions-bonuses\">Conditions, Afflictions, &amp; Bonuses</a><ul><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-advantage-and-disadvantage\">Advantage and Disadvantage</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-focus-and-concentration\">Focus and Concentration</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-status-conditions\">Status Conditions</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-bodily-harm-table\">Bodily Harm Table</a></li><li><a href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-bodily-disaster-table\">Bodily Disaster Table</a></li></ul></li></ul></nav><h2 id=\"rules-introduction\">Introduction<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>&emsp;Welcome to Endolore. Please make the most of your savage, desperate, and short life here.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Bloodsoup Hell is a highly collaborative and improvisational eldritch-horror meat-grinder TTRPG. What that means is don\u2019t come prepared for anything but to think on your feet and be a team player in a strange, funny, and violent adventure.</p>\n<p>&emsp;All major characters in the game, both player and non-player alike, as well as many of the settings and items, will be randomized or created on the spot with the help of each player at the table using a rotational attribute selection mechanic that should take no more than five minutes at per character. Each player will have the opportunity to shape not only the world itself but their fellow players\u2019 characters and the antagonists they face.</p>\n<p>&emsp;The world of Endolore does not share the physical or biological properties of the sane; it is an inside-out planet, and not a planet at all. There is no outside, and no earth; there is only the Meat, an enigmatic sheet of living flesh that surrounds the entire knowable world. Everything in the world emerges fully formed from the Meat, and the Meat pulls everything inexorably back to its embrace with its gravitational force. In other words, this is both the geometrically outermost and gravitationally lowest layer of the world.</p>\n<p>&emsp;In contrast, the Sea sits at the geometrical core and gravitationally highest point of Endolore, the 32 Word Rivers flowing outward and down from it in all directions, disappearing into the Void Channels in the Meat. Nobody has followed the cold, salty water down into the Void Channels and been seen or heard from again.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Very few, if any, people in Endolore have ever seen the Sea; but very many have attempted to follow one of the World Rivers up through the layers between it and the Meat in an attempt to escape this hell realm and the strange, grimdark gods who torment them; most hold the very real hope that though they may die trying in this life, the spirit of their party and the foundations they lay will carry a future incarnation of them to victory.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Between the Meat and the Sea sit an enormous variety of environments and challenges. People are, of course, not the only thing that emerges from the Meat; it also extrudes many bizarre and horrible monsters, as well as a prodigious amount of eerie and beautiful structures. Buildings, landscapes, labyrinths, etc; all kinds of inexplicable architecture both natural-looking and seemingly-manmade is constantly emerging from the Meat in an infinite stack that rises up all the way to the Sea (where presumably it is washed away into the World Rivers).</p>\n<p>&emsp;Because of the constant churn of stacking structures, the content of each layer is always changing; one thing that remains consistent is that the newer structures sit closer to the Meat, and the older structures \u2013 ones that have been around for thousands of years \u2013 sit near the Sea. And the closer to the Sea you get, the fewer living beings you tend to find, but the more perilous the challenges.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-the-coriander-system\">The Coriander System<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>Coriander is a Tabletop RPG character sheet management system used by Bloodsoup Hell and its sister game, Wizards of New Tabulaera. It is designed as a general purpose fantasy RPG system and an alternative to the traditional character sheet formats provided by games like Dungeons &amp; Dragons and Pathfinder. The generic form of the system spreadsheet is open source and free to modify or use as is with any setting or other ruleset.</p>\n<p>Its purpose is to provide robust, dynamic, and mathematically complex character stats and ability scores without sacrificing ease of use. It accomplishes this by relying on either a companion app [in development] or a digital spreadsheet so that all of the tedious math involved in character creation is done automatically under the hood on an ongoing basis. It is intended to be used to create a TTRPG that is mechanically complex and dynamic without relying on things like class abilities, level-specific features, or other attributes that weigh down gameplay.</p>\n<p>The core system handles character Experience Points (ExP), Level, Class, Skill Modifiers, and Stat Block, all of which are described in this section; the rules for gameplay, as well as things like items, playable races, and other aspects of character creation are left to the core rules or setting of the game to specify. The Endolore setting uses an amended version of the Coriander spreadsheet which tracks certain stat-based equations and other trackable values provided by the setting rules covered in the next sections.</p>\n<p>In the Coriander system, nearly all agents in the world, particularly player characters and their antagonists, are measured in what they can do by Adventure Points (AvP). AvP are acquired by gaining Experience Points (ExP), an agent\u2019s Level is a measure of how many AvP it has, and AvP are cashed in to buy stronger ability scores.</p>\n<p>There are two ways to increase your character\u2019s abilities using AvP: Talent Points (TaP) and Proficiency Points (PrP), both of which affect your Skill Modifiers in different ways. TaP increase 8 different modifiers by +1, and PrP increase 1 modifier by +4; each TaP costs 2 AvP, and each PrP costs 1 AvP. For both types, all a player has to do is input the value they want for any given Talent or Proficiency and the spreadsheet will total the corresponding values and apply them to the Skill Modifiers.</p>\n<p>There are 9 Talents and 36 Proficiencies, corresponding to the 36 Skill Modifiers they generate. Generally, a Talent may have any value between 1 and 20, and a Proficiency starts at a value of 0 and can go up to 10.</p>\n<p>A character that has 10 TaP in each Talent and no PrP will have a score of 0 for every Skill Modifier, meaning you would not add or subtract anything from your dice roll when using any given Skill; this corresponds to having 180 AvP, which is the average for what is known as a Commoner, or Level 0 character. Many of the characters your Player Characters will see and interact with will be Level 0, but most Player Characters (PCs), even at the beginning of the game, will be a cut above the category of Commoner.</p>\n<p>Skill Modifiers are the primary measure of a character\u2019s abilities. When your character takes a challenging action and you make a Skill Check by rolling dice to determine how successful or not the action is, you add (or subtract if it\u2019s negative) one of your character\u2019s Skill Modifiers to the result of the roll; the higher the result the better, so naturally the higher the Skill Modifier the more likely you are to succeed.</p>\n<p>The 36 different Skill Modifiers are as follows:</p>\n<p>Acrobatics: body flexibility, parkour, etc.</p>\n<p>Carrying: how much mass your body is built to lug</p>\n<p>Grappling: subduing enemies, snatching, catching, etc.</p>\n<p>Estimation: gauging physical size and weight, hitting a target with arrows</p>\n<p>Battlewise: knowledge of enemy weaknesses, technique, etc.</p>\n<p>Fitness: general bodily health and stress tolerance</p>\n<p>Intimidate: making something scared of you</p>\n<p>Athletics: long distance running, endurance activities, melee fighting</p>\n<p>Guard: ability to mitigate incoming damage</p>\n<p>Stealth: sneaking, theft, etc.</p>\n<p>Reflex: avoiding surprise attacks and traps</p>\n<p>Medicine: healing people non-magically, biology, etc.</p>\n<p>Recovery: ability to come back strong from damage or stress</p>\n<p>Performance: impressions, entertainment, etc.</p>\n<p>Clockwork: Lockpicking, manipulating small and fragile objects</p>\n<p>Gossip: ability to gather general information from the public</p>\n<p>D. Awareness: sense of impending danger, of particularly poor choices</p>\n<p>Religion: knowledge of and relations with gods and high culture</p>\n<p>Resistance: surviving poisoning, damage, etc.</p>\n<p>Bluff: making something believe you to be stronger or weaker</p>\n<p>Courage: ability to face a difficult situation head-on</p>\n<p>Investigation: searching for clues, loot, important things, tasting the dirt</p>\n<p>Knowledge: breadth of general information and written comprehension</p>\n<p>Handling: Taming animals, commanding mounts, familiars, summons</p>\n<p>Diplomacy: making somebody like you</p>\n<p>Tracking: anything from keeping arrow sight to closely following</p>\n<p>Nature: connection to the plants and planet</p>\n<p>S. Awareness: clear perception of one\u2019s own body and mind</p>\n<p>Insight: comprehension of others\u2019 unstated intentions</p>\n<p>Arcana: understanding of magic and magical things</p>\n<p>Survival: hunting food, camping, finding mushrooms, etc.</p>\n<p>Deception: anything from general misleading to outright lying</p>\n<p>History: informational recall, general life experience</p>\n<p>Soundness: staying collected under stress, outward trustworthiness, etc.</p>\n<p>Mentality: resistance to mental status conditions, psychic attacks, etc.</p>\n<p>Persuasion: making somebody agree with you</p>\n<p>Your Skill Modifiers will determine the scores in your Stat Block, discussed below, as well as your Class. A character\u2019s Class has no mechanical value in the Coriander system, but rather serves as a way to categorize the many Skills and illustrate what your character is good at and the role they may play in their party. A game\u2019s setting may or may not include functions tied to Class in its mechanics (Tabulaera does not). There are 4 Classes, each with 3 Subclasses; each Subclass is represented by the total of the values of 3 Skill Modifiers, and your character\u2019s Class and Subclass are determined by whichever total is highest.</p>\n<p>There are some broader aspects of your character that are important to keep track of, but are not covered by the Skill Modifiers on their own \u2013 such as vitality, stamina, and how well they can hit a target. These are covered in the Stat Block, and there are twelve categories: HP, SP, EP, RD, IB, AP, SA, DA, BP, AC, CC, and WC. They are calculated through the way various values of your skill modifiers relate to each other (don\u2019t worry, the character sheet performs all of these calculations for you as you enter your AvP); their applications are explained in more detail by the setting ruleset, as they are some of the primary factors of how your character performs while adventuring.</p>\n<p>HP (Health Points) How much damage you can absorb without dying</p>\n<p>SP (Stamina Points) How much you can exert yourself in the short term</p>\n<p>RD (Run Distance) How fast you can move on foot</p>\n<p>IB (Initiative Bonus) How quickly you\u2019re able to take action</p>\n<p>AP (Action Points) How many actions you can perform in a few seconds</p>\n<p>SA (Strike Accuracy) How well you\u2019re able to hit a target you aim at</p>\n<p>DA (Damage Avoidance) How well you\u2019re able to avoid an attack</p>\n<p>BP (Base Power) How much damage you deal with a physical attack</p>\n<p>AC (Armor Class) How much damage you absorb from a physical attack</p>\n<p>CC (Carrying Capacity) How much weight you can carry</p>\n<p>EP (Endurance Points) How much you can exert yourself in the long term</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-starting-the-game\">Starting the Game<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>To start the first session of a campaign, all players at the table will work together to create PCs (Player Characters) for everyone except the QM (Quest Master) using the process laid out below. This should take no more than 5 minutes per player.</p>\n<p>Bloodsoup Hell uses the standard Coriander character stat block system as well as coins and standard sets of 4-, 6-, 8-, 10-, 12-, and 20-sided dice (henceforth referred to as DX, where \u201cX\u201d is the number of sides).</p>\n<p>To begin character creation, each player who will have a PC creates a blank character stat block and rolls a D20. The QM creates an initiative order for the players based on the number they rolled, with the highest number first and the lowest number last.</p>\n<ul><li>the first player in initiative describes the physical appearance of the character in 1-3 sentences. </li><li>Then, the next player in initiative rolls 2D20 to select 2 abilities from the Abilities list, and picks 1 of those abilities to assign to the character.</li><li>Then, the next player in initiative rolls 2D8 to select 2 fighting styles and pick 1 of those fighting styles to assign to the character.</li><li>Then, the next player in initiative rolls 2D12 to select 2 weapons and pick 1 of those weapons to assign to the character.</li><li>Then, the next player in initiative describes the character\u2019s backstory and motivation in 1-3 sentences. </li><li>Finally, in initiative order, players each add +2 to one Talent and subtract -1 from another talent, then add a +1 to 1 Proficiency, until this process has been repeated four times.</li></ul>\n<p>Repeat this process until a character has been created for each player. The QM\u2019s job here is mostly to facilitate the rotation and pace of the character creation, but they also have sole veto power if something a player assigns to a character doesn\u2019t fit their vision for the game.</p>\n<p>Once all characters have been created, the players roll another D20 and the QM puts them in order from highest roll to lowest roll. The player with the highest roll chooses a name for the first character that was created, and that becomes their character. This process repeats for the second highest roll and the second character created, etc., all the way to the lowest roll and the last character created.</p>\n<p>Then, the group can decide where to begin their story. Advanced players might choose to begin at a later point (see below), but the standard starting place for all characters is their emergence from the Meat.</p>\n<p>All characters are born from the Meat at the age they\u2019re intended to be played at, be it teenager, adult, or elder; there are no parents in Endolore, and no childhoods. Many people emerge from the Meat with amnesia; some know only the rules of this world and others know nothing at all. Many others, however, may have distant memories of their lives in other worlds \u2014 families, knighthoods, professorships, riches, poverty, religion, etc. \u2014 there are those who believe these people are here in death as a punishment for their sins in life; that they were banished here by the dark magic of an enemy; or that their memories are simply an illusion and this is the only world they really know. Only the Greater Gods who live in the outer worlds between universes could truly say; and to be told anything by them would be to hear things no mortal is equipped to comprehend.</p>\n<p>The natural thing to do at this point is for the players to begin role playing their awakening in Endolore. The QM should describe their emergence from the Meat into the Wild \u2014 the dark space outside the structures that make up the world. It is suggested to introduce a hostile creature for them to fight as soon as they begin, in order to give them a reason to come together and introduce them to the nature of the world.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-items-and-magic\">Items and Magic<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>When a character first emerges from the Meat, they have nothing but their clothes, their weapon, and a single other item. You decide what that item is when your character takes it out to use it; until you have declared what it is, it is listed in your inventory as a General Purpose Item. The General Purpose Item can be anything as long as it is mundane, small enough to be easily carried, and not particularly valuable \u2014 10 m of rope, a collapsible fishing rod, a bag of ball bearings, a torch \u2014 but once you have declared what it is, that\u2019s what you have and you can\u2019t change it to something else later; so wait to decide what it is until you need it!</p>\n<p>For better or worse, everyone in Endolore is capable of using magic. Most commonly, this will be done through the use of a spellbook, enchanted item, or potion. Magic can only be channeled through specific materials: namely certain kinds of woods, metals, or minerals, fungi, and their derivatives.</p>\n<p>A Spellbook is an item that allows its user to use magic with their own vital energy as its fuel source. A spellbook will contain the runes needed to compose 1-5 spells; each spell in a book will have its own Key Page which allows all of the thousands of runes that form the spell to activate and bring it to life. In order to cast a spell from a spellbook, the user must be in physical contact with the spellbook and read the runes directly from the Key Page.</p>\n<p>An enchanted item is an item that has been forged with magic power to condense all of the thousands of runes needed to create a spell down into a single key phrase, marked somewhere on the item (drawing a few runes on something does not enchant it, the process is more like baking an entire spellbook into it). These items are most often made of magic-conductive wood or metal, but can also be stone or mixed materials, and they usually contain a gemstone that can be used to gradually syphon energy from the world around and store it as the magic\u2019s primary fuel source. Most enchanted items also require the user to Attune to them by meditating with them for a period of time before they can be used.</p>\n<p>Potions are created through a process called Alchemy, which involves infusing energy into a consumable material to create a one time use effect, and they often use mushrooms as one of their key ingredients and require their user to ingest them to gain their effects.</p>\n<p>There are other ways of using magic besides these; for example, monsters are born with magical properties as part of their biology, and the gods tap directly into the Aspects that make all magic possible as the source of their power. A person can also get runes tattooed on their body through a process similar to enchanting an item (though much more dangerous), becoming effectively a living spellbook! Most forms of magic are, however, fairly rare and highly coveted in Endolore.</p>\n<p>All magic regardless of its type will be provided with: a name; AP, SP, EP, and other costs for use; area of effect and duration; and a description of its effect(s).</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-character-death-and-reincarnation\">Character Death and Reincarnation<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>Death is commonplace in worlds such as Endolore, and must be dealt with on an ongoing basis. When a being\u2019s HP is reduced to less than 0, it is dead; expect this to happen many times during your campaign. The dead of Endolore, however, have special and well-known properties.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-spiritual-guidance\">Spiritual Guidance<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>When a being with a profound soul dies, their spirit remains with those who remember them until they are reincarnated. A player whose character has died and has not yet been reincarnated can use the Spiritual Guidance ability.</p>\n<p>A dead character cannot gain ET, but they can still use Spiritual Guidance to expend any ET they had in reserve when they died to add them to another player\u2019s roll.</p>\n<p>Additionally, up to a total of 3 times, they can use Spiritual Guidance to either give another player Advantage on an eligible roll or add their relevant modifier to another character\u2019s modifier for an Attack roll or Skill Check; the number of uses of this ability a player has resets when their character is reincarnated.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-reincarnation\">Reincarnation<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>When a being is reincarnated, part of its soul is imbued into a new being that is formed by the Meat, and the rest of its soul becomes one with the Aspects and is returned to the Cycle.</p>\n<p>PCs can spend 1 hour praying to the Meat and offer the spirit of a fallen comrade as a sacrifice, and the Meat will appear to them wherever they are and produce a reincarnation of the spirit in the form of a new PC. At this time, role play is paused and the players return to the character creation mechanic to build a new character for the reincarnated player to control.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-totem-and-legacy-tokens\">Totem and Legacy Tokens<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>In life, a character can collect two special tokens that affect their reincarnation after death: a Totem Token and a Legacy Token; besides ET for Spiritual Guidance, these are the only tokens a spirit retains after death.</p>\n<p>A Totem Token can be obtained through feats of courage and valor, such as overcoming a deadly obstacle or defeating a powerful enemy. A Legacy Token is earned by honoring the character\u2019s goals and values laid out in their backstory, or by moments of exceptionally creative or moving role play. A character can only have up to 1 of each of these tokens, and their distribution is at the sole discretion of the QM.</p>\n<p>If a character has a Totem Token and/or a Legacy Token when they are reincarnated, the tokens are expended and converted into a Totem Bonus and/or Legacy Bonus for their reincarnation during character creation.</p>\n<p>A Totem Bonus adds all of the Talent and Proficiency modifiers (both positive and negative) added in the creation of the character who obtained the token to the beneficiary of the bonus. A Legacy Bonus adds the Ability added during the creation of the character who obtained the token to the beneficiary of the bonus. Both of these are in addition to the modifiers/abilities added during the new character\u2019s creation; a character\u2019s Totem/Legacy bonuses stack and are passed on to each subsequent reincarnation for the duration of the campaign.</p>\n<p>The QM has the right to revoke 1 Totem OR Legacy Token upon character death, <em>at their sole discretion</em>, <strong>if and only if</strong> they strongly feel (or explicitly know) that the player died intentionally to get a new/stronger character or for similarly unsporting reasons (this rule is not intended to be invoked more than once with any given player, and ideally not at all; if it must be, it is advised to consider whether or not the player is a good fit for the table.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-abilities-table\">Abilities Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><td>1</td><td>Meat Matron</td><td>When you die, you may expend 1 Totem Token or 1 Legacy Token to cause up to 2 other dead characters in your party to reincarnate immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Battle Versatility&emsp;</td><td>Roll a fighting style; you are treated as having this fighting style in addition to any others you have.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Prepared For the Worst&emsp;</td><td>You start with 3 general purpose items instead of 1.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Bat Out Of Hell&emsp;</td><td>You can spend 4 SP to move twice your RD in a single move action.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Explosive Personality&emsp;</td><td>You gain 1 ET each time you rest.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Always So Defensive&emsp;</td><td>Once per action, you can expend 1 ET to reroll a 1 when making a skill check against an enemy\u2019s ability.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>No Sell&emsp;</td><td>You can expend 8 EP to negate the damage done to you by a single hit.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Rolling In Their Graves&emsp;</td><td>Other players have a total of 3 free uses of spiritual guidance on you while you\u2019re alive. When you\u2019re dead, you may use spiritual guidance 5 times instead of 3</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Lazy&emsp;</td><td>The benefits you gain from resting are doubled, but your AP and RD are reduced by -20%</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Indecisive&emsp;</td><td>Roll 2 abilities and select 1; you are treated as though you have that ability instead of this one until you are reincarnated.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Spell Duelist&emsp;</td><td>You can equip a spellbook and open it to a Key Page as a single action for 2 AP.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Making the Dream Work&emsp;</td><td>When you make a Skill Check to initiate a Combo Attack, you can expend 1 ET to reduce the AP cost by -1, to a minimum of 1.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Lucky&emsp;</td><td>Any time you roll a die and don\u2019t like the result, you may expend 3 ET to reroll it.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Perfectionist&emsp;</td><td>Your Perfect Combo range is increased by +1 if you are the Leader; e.g. if it was a difference of 0, it becomes 0-1. This supersedes but does not change your Critical Combo range.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Critical Reave Theory&emsp;</td><td>The maximum of your Critical Combo range is increased by +1 if you are the Leader; e.g. if it was a difference of 1-3, it becomes 1-4.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Survivor&emsp;</td><td>If you are the only living member of your party, all of your aggregate stats are increased by 25% and you may expend 3 ET and 3 AP to restore your HP by 25% as an action for the duration.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Shamanic Shuffle&emsp;</td><td>When another member of your party is reincarnated, you may swap this ability with one of their Legacy Bonuses (they now have this ability and you now have the one you selected).</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Gone But Not Forgotten</td><td>If another character in your party is dead, you may select one of their Legacy Bonuses; you are treated as having that ability until they are reincarnated.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Forgotten But Not Gone&emsp;</td><td>When you die, you may expend a Totem Token or a Legacy Token to reincarnate immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Highly Capable</td><td>Roll 2 abilities; you are treated as though you have both of them instead of this ability until you are reincarnated.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"rules-fighting-styles-table\">Fighting Styles Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><td>1</td><td>Defender</td><td>Add +2 to your CR when blocking or parrying; you make rolls to hit with a D10 instead of a D12.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Keen</td><td>Your critical hit threshold is reduced by -3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Scrapper</td><td>You have advantage to hit any combatant that hit you since the end of your last turn</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Janitor</td><td>You have advantage to hit any combatant that has been hit since the end of your last turn</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Mobile</td><td>You can expend 2 SP to take the shift action for 0 AP; cannot be used if your last action was a shift action</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Flashy</td><td>Any time you use an ET on an attack or damage roll, it is treated as 2 ET.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Quickdraw</td><td>Your AP cost for equipping a spellbook and accessing the keyrune is reduced by -1.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Ambusher</td><td>You have advantage to hit any combatant that is surprised or doesn\u2019t detect you.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"rules-weapons-table\">Weapons Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><td>1</td><td>Dagger</td><td>7</td><td>Hunting Bow</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Shortsword</td><td>8</td><td>Mace</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Longsword</td><td>9</td><td>Warhammer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Greatsword</td><td>10</td><td>Spear</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Shortbow</td><td>11</td><td>Glaive</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td></td><td>12</td><td></td></tr></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"rules-weapon-types\">Weapon Types<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<p>Type: Dagger</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Light Melee</p>\n<p>Weight: 1</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 0-1</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+1D4</p>\n<p>Effect: If you make a Weapon Attack as an Action using a different Light Weapon with your other hand, you can make a Weapon Attack using this weapon as a Reaction for 1 AP.</p>\n<p>Type: Shortsword</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Light Melee</p>\n<p>Weight: 2</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 0-1</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 1D4</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+2+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: none</p>\n<p>Type: Longsword</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Balanced Melee</p>\n<p>Weight: 4</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 1</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 1+1D4</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+3+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: none</p>\n<p>Type: Greatsword</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Heavy Melee</p>\n<p>Weight: 7</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 1</p>\n<p>Cost: 3 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 2+1D2</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+4+1D8</p>\n<p>Effect: Must be wielded with two hands</p>\n<p>Type: Shortbow</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Light Projectile</p>\n<p>Weight: 1</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 5-20</p>\n<p>Cost: 3 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: Must be used with 2 hands. You can only Attack with this weapon as a Readied Action. Attacking with this weapon expends 1 arrow. You can make a Weapon Attack at half or double range with disadvantage to hit.</p>\n<p>Type: Hunting Bow</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Balanced Projectile</p>\n<p>Weight: 2</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 5-15</p>\n<p>Cost: 3 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+3+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: Must be used with 2 hands. You can only Attack with this weapon as a Readied Action. Attacking with this weapon expends 1 arrow. You can make a Weapon Attack at half or double range with disadvantage to hit.</p>\n<p>Type: Mace</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Balanced Melee</p>\n<p>Weight: 4</p>\n<p>Damage: Blunt</p>\n<p>Range: 0-1</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 1D4</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: Ignores up to 3 AC</p>\n<p>Type: Warhammer</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Heavy Mid-Range</p>\n<p>Weight: 10</p>\n<p>Damage: Blunt</p>\n<p>Range: 1-2</p>\n<p>Cost: 4 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+1D10</p>\n<p>Effect: Must be wielded with two hands. Ignores up to 5 AC.</p>\n<p>Type: Spear</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Light Mid-Range</p>\n<p>Weight: 2</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 1-2</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>WP: BP+2+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: You can make a Weapon Attack treating this weapon as a Projectile with a range of 3-10 by throwing it. It is then removed from your inventory and must be retrieved to be used again.</p>\n<p>Type: Glaive</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Balanced Mid-Range</p>\n<p>Weight: 4</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 1-2</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 1D4</p>\n<p>WP: BP+3+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: Must be wielded with two hands. If a target enters your range with this weapon, you can make a Weapon Attack against it as a Reaction for 1 AP.</p>\n<p>Type: Pike</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Light Mid-Range</p>\n<p>Weight: 3</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 1-2</p>\n<p>Cost: 2 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 1D4</p>\n<p>WP: BP+1D6</p>\n<p>Effect: If a target enters your range with this weapon, you can make a Weapon Attack against it as a Reaction for 1 AP.</p>\n<p>Type: Crossbow</p>\n<p>Size/Style: Heavy Projectile</p>\n<p>Weight: 10</p>\n<p>Damage: Sharp</p>\n<p>Range: 5-15</p>\n<p>Cost: 3 AP</p>\n<p>CR: 0</p>\n<p>Dmg: BP+4+1D10</p>\n<p>Effect: You can only attack with this weapon if it\u2019s armed; you may spend an action to arm it for 5 AP. Attacking with this weapon expends 1 crossbow bolt. You can make a Weapon Attack at half or double range with disadvantage to hit. Disadvantage to hit if used with one hand.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-interacting-with-the-world\">Interacting With the World<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"rules-skill-checks-and-assists\">Skill Checks and Assists<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;When you choose to take an action for which there is doubt that your character would be able to pull it off on the first try, or when you are defending against an effect that a special skill can be applied to, you must perform a Skill Check to see how well you manage it. For a Skill Check, the QM decides which one of the 36 skills is most relevant to the action being attempted and sets a Challenge Rating (CR) for the specific action being attempted; you roll a D20, add your modifier for the specified skill to the result, and then subtract the CR of the action from that. If the total result is 0 or higher, you succeed; otherwise, you fail.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Because Skill Checks can be applied to such a wide variety of situations and the possible outcomes of those situations can vary so widely in turn, the results of any given Skill Check should be something the QM does on a case-by-case basis. However, a good rule of thumb is this when comparing your modified roll against the CR: </p>\n<ul><li>if the total is 0-4, it is a narrow success and you achieve a bare minimum of your goal, possibly with complications;</li><li>if the total is 5-9, it is a moderate success and you achieve your goal comfortably, but no more than that;</li><li>if the total is 10+, it is a Critical Success and you achieve all possible success parameters, possibly with an additional boon;</li><li>if the total is negative 1-4, it is a narrow failure and you miss your mark, but may have a chance at redemption;</li><li>if the total is negative 5-9, it is a moderate failure and you do not achieve your goal, but nothing catastrophic happens;</li><li>and if the total is negative 10 or below, it is a Critical Failure and you fail completely to achieve your goal, possibly with other negative repercussions.</li></ul>\n<p>&emsp;Critical Success and Failure are discussed in more depth below.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If a character is preparing to perform a Skill Check, another character can sometimes perform an Assist. In order to do so, the assisting character must be able to enter a state of Focus or Concentration (discussed in the Conditions, Afflictions, and Bonuses section) for the duration of the Skill Check; depending on the task, they may also need to be within arm\u2019s reach or earshot of the character they are assisting, and/or have at least one free hand (e.g. you can\u2019t assist with lifting a crate if you\u2019re wrestling an enemy on the other side of the room). By default the effect of multiple Assists are cumulative; however, the QM may say that no Assists are possible on any given task, or may set limits on the number of people able to Assist in a task.</p>\n<p>&emsp;When you attempt to Assist another character, roll a Skill Check using the same modifier as the main Skill Check but against a CR equal to 10+ the relevant skill modifier of the character you\u2019re trying to Assist, prior to the target character rolling their Skill Check. If you succeed, they can add half of your modifier to their own in the Check you are Assisting them in; if you have a Critical Success, they also have advantage on their roll. If you fail the roll, they make the check with a -3 penalty; if you fail Critically, they also have disadvantage on the check.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-passive-skills\">Passive Skills<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;If a character makes a skill check against another character\u2019s skill when the target is not actively making an effort to stop them (e.g. a stealth check to pickpocket), the target uses her passive value for that skill. The passive value is the skill\u2019s modifier +5. If the player has stated beforehand that they are on guard for a specific eventuality and there is a justifiable reason for it (e.g. you are walking in a crowded city thoroughfare and you say you are on guard for pickpockets because this is where they like to strike), the passive value for any relevant skill to contest such an occurrence becomes the relevant skill modifier +10.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-critical-success-and-critical-failure\">Critical Success and Critical Failure<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;There are times when a character will wildly succeed or fail at an endeavor, and her efforts may be rewarded or punished accordingly; these times are determined partly by the character\u2019s skill and partly by chance.</p>\n<p>&emsp;When you roll a die for a Skill Check or an Attack roll and the result is the highest number (e.g. 20 on a D20), you add a +5 to your modifier for that roll, representing a stroke of luck, flash of insight, etc. Likewise, when you roll a 1, you subtract -5 from your modifier, representing a misfortune, momentary slip of focus, etc.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Attack rolls and combat are discussed in depth in the Encounters section.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If your total to perform a check or hit a target is 10 or more greater than the CR for the task or the target\u2019s DA, you have a Critical Success. In the case of a Skill Check, you are given the maximum possible reward for the check (e.g. an investigation would uncover all relevant information there is to find in the searched area), and you may be given an additional boon at the QM\u2019s discretion. In the case of an Attack roll, your total damage dealt after dice rolls and AC calculation is doubled (make sure to subtract the target\u2019s AC <em>first</em>), and the target has disadvantage on any Skill Checks it must make as part of the attack\u2019s Effect.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If your total to perform a check or hit a target is 10 or more less than the CR for the task or the target\u2019s DA, you have a Critical Failure. In the case of a Skill Check, you gain nothing as a result of the check and may suffer an additional penalty at the QM\u2019s discretion. In the case of an Attack roll, not only does the Attack miss but you fail to recover 2 AP at the start of your next turn.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-explode-tokens\">Explode Tokens<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;There are a variety of situations in which a player or group may be rewarded with an Explode Token (ET). The party might receive some as a reward in leu of or in addition to ExP for handling a random encounter well, or the QM might (if inclined) give an individual player 1-3 ET for role playing their character in a compelling way.</p>\n<p>&emsp;A character can have up to 10 ET at a time, and the player can spend as many as they want anytime they roll a die for any reason. You must declare that you are spending them and how many you will spend before you roll and cannot change the number after rolling, but if rolling multiple dice you may choose which dice to apply each token to after rolling.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Each ET that is applied to a die effectively increases the number rolled on that die by 1. This effect can be stacked as many times as you like up to the die\u2019s maximum number. If an ET is applied to a die that is on its maximum number, that die \u201cexplodes\u201d: take that number and roll another die of that type, adding it to the total. Each die can only be exploded one time, but you do not have to apply all ET spent before rolling exploded dice.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Examples:</p>\n<ul><li>You declare that you are using 2 ET on a D20 roll. You roll an 8; because of the 2 ET, you count the result of that roll as a 10.</li><li>You declare that you are using 1 ET on a D12 roll. You roll a 12; because you have applied an ET to a die that landed on its maximum number, it explodes, and you roll a second D12. This time you roll a 4; you add both results together, making your total for this roll 16.</li><li>You declare that you are using 3 ET on a D10 roll. You roll a 9; the first ET you\u2019ve applied makes the result of that die a 10, and because it is now counted as being on its maximum number, the second ET causes it to explode; you roll a second D10 and get a 6, and because you still have an ET to apply it is applied to that die, making it a 7; your total for this roll is 17.</li><li>You declare that you are using 4 ET on a D8 roll. You roll an 8; your first ET causes the die to explode and you roll another D8. This time, you roll a 6; your second and third ET bump this number up to an 8, so the last ET causes that die to explode and you roll a third D8. This time, you roll a 5. Your total for this roll is 21.</li><li>You declare that you are using 5 ET on a 3D6 roll. You roll a 3, a 5, and a 6. You decide to apply 2 ET to the die that landed on 5 and 1 ET to the die that landed on 6, causing both of them to explode; you roll an additional 2D6. This time, you roll a 2 and a 4; because you still have 2 ET to apply, you decide to apply 1 to each die, making them 3 and 5. Your total for this roll is 23.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"rules-travel-rest-and-sustenance\">Travel, Rest, and Sustenance<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"rules-long-distance-travel-and-rest\">Long Distance Travel and Rest<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;While time spent actually role playing in the game happens in (more or less) real time, the less exciting parts of characters\u2019 lives like sleeping and travel can be compressed into brief narrative and formula. When traveling on foot, as a general rule your party can travel a distance equal to the slowest member\u2019s RD x10 in miles if they really push themselves; for each of your character\u2019s RD you travel in miles, you lose 1 EP. The EP cost of traveling mounted or with a vehicle will be described in the mount/vehicle\u2019s properties.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If your character loses half her EP, she is in a state of Exhaustion, which, among other things, makes long distance travel even harder. This is discussed in more detail in the Conditions, Afflictions, and Bonuses section. Carrying more than your character\u2019s maximum Carrying Capacity (CC) also causes her to lose EP twice as quickly during long distance travel.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Sometimes your characters will need to relax and recover from their adventure. This also takes place almost entirely in a condensed format, with different intervals of time the players can choose to rest causing different effects on the characters.</p>\n<p><em>&emsp;Respite</em></p>\n<p>&emsp;Duration: 1 hour</p>\n<p>&emsp;Effect: You can roll a Med Check to make and apply a Salve if you are Trained in it. You recover 1D2 HP and 1D2-1 EP; this effect cannot be used again until you recover at least 2 EP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<em>Short Rest</em></p>\n<p><em>&emsp;</em>Duration: 4 hours</p>\n<p>&emsp;Effect: You can sleep for up to 3 hours and/or eat a full meal if you wish. You recover 1D4+1 HP and 1D2+1 EP; this effect cannot be used again until you recover at least 6 EP,</p>\n<p>&emsp;<em>Full Rest</em> </p>\n<p>&emsp;Duration: 12 hours</p>\n<p>&emsp;Effect: You can set up and take down camp, eat two full meals, and/or have a full sleep. You recover 1D4+10% of your total HP and 2D2+2 EP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<em>Long Rest</em></p>\n<p>&emsp;Duration: 1 full day and 2 nights</p>\n<p>&emsp;Effect: You recover 1D8+30% of your total HP and 1D6+6 EP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<em>Extended Rest</em></p>\n<p>&emsp;Duration: 3 full days and 4 nights</p>\n<p>&emsp;Effect: Your HP and EP are restored to full.</p>\n<p>&emsp;When your characters are not staying in some sort of accommodations, they must set up camp. If you have a camping kit, it takes an hour to set up. Setting up a fire also takes an hour in addition to the sleeping arrangement (if you <em>have</em> a flint and steel), and many foods require a camping kit to cook on a campfire. Packing the camp up in the morning also takes an additional hour.</p>\n<p>&emsp;It is possible that your group will be accosted by enemies, capricious entities, or wild beasts no matter where you camp. In highly dangerous places, it is a good idea to set up a watch in order to reduce the likelihood of a random encounter catching the party by surprise. The watch usually happens automatically, but the hours your character is on watch don\u2019t count toward your total hours slept. Under certain circumstances, like if your character is suffering from exhaustion, the QM can make you roll a soundness check to stay awake during your shift. If you fail and fall asleep, it can mean the remainder of the shifts for the night don\u2019t take place.</p>\n<p>&emsp;While traveling or resting, a character is largely bound either to the circumstances of locomotion or to relaxation, but there are a few activities they can choose to perform during this time, which may be beneficial to the game or simply for the enrichment of the character. For example, character with high Charisma could play a game of cards with an NPC while riding in a wagon, and use the game to help get information she wants or gain her trust; or, an aspiring poet could practice poetry just for the love of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-encounters\">Encounters<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"rules-turn-based-time\">Turn-Based Time<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;Encounters are the most detailed part of an adventure. They are key moments of decisive action and include things like skill challenges, combat, and time-sensitive puzzles. All encounters are turn based action. Typically, they are events that last between 12 seconds and 1 minute, with each round composing 6 seconds.</p>\n<p>&emsp;In a round, each actor involved in the encounter takes a turn, which is a description of what they do in the time frame of the round. The turn order is determined by an Initiative roll, in which the turn order is the score of each actor in descending order, determined by a D12 roll and any effects that modify the conditions or results of said roll, most notably the Initiative Bonus (IB), which is added to the D12 results of every Initiative roll. </p>\n<p>&emsp;If two actors get the same total in their Initiative roll after adding modifiers, the involved parties roll against each other with no modifiers and the higher scores of those go before the lower.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If a being gets a total of 5 or more points higher than the second highest score on its Initiative roll, it has Critical Initiative; it has an additional +3 AP for the first round of the encounter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-action-points\">Action Points<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;The most basic factor of all encounters in the Coriander system is each actor\u2019s Action Points (AP), which are found in the Aggregate Stat Block. Your character\u2019s AP is a measure of how much you can do in one round of an encounter. The average commoner has 6 AP, and most player characters start with 6-8, so because a round is 6 seconds long (and although it\u2019s not intended to be hyper-realistic), you could think of each point as representing what a normal person could do in a single second.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Each Action you can take in an encounter has an AP cost, aside from Free Actions; most standard Actions take 2 or 3 AP, and all Actions described in the rules of the game or the properties of a being, item, or effect will include an AP cost as part of the description \u2013 if the action you wish to take is not described as part of the game but your QM allows you to take it, the QM will determine the AP cost. </p>\n<p>&emsp;During your turn, you may take any Action as long as your current AP is equal to or greater than the AP cost of the Action; when you begin taking the Action, your current AP is reduced by the Action\u2019s cost; you may not take an Action if it is not your turn. Free Actions, such as speaking, do not reduce your current AP and can be taken at any time (unless a specific trigger is specified or it is part of a larger effect) whether or not you have AP remaining. Reactions, including those that interrupt other effects, can be taken at any time whether or not it is your turn, but have an AP cost with the same limitations as Actions, and always have a triggering condition that must be met to use them.</p>\n<p>&emsp;You may, but do not have to, use all of your AP in a round. Regardless of how much of your AP you use, your current AP is restored to full at the beginning of each of your turns.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you know what your character is going to do before your turn begins and declare all of your Actions without hesitation or deliberation, you may treat your character as having 1 additional AP until the start of your next turn.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-stamina-use-and-recovery\">Stamina Use and Recovery<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;In addition to AP, many actions used in an encounter also require SP. Many individual actions have their own SP requirements in their descriptions; those that do not specify an SP cost do not reduce your current SP when you take them.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If your current SP becomes 0, you enter the Winded condition, which is described in the Conditions, Afflictions, and Bonuses subsection. If an action would cost more SP than you currently have by 3 points or less, you perform the action as normal; if it would cost more SP than you currently have by more than 3 points, you must make an Ath, Fit, or Cou check with a CR of (SP cost)x5 or the action fails; in any case, you are reduced to 0 SP. </p>\n<p>&emsp;At the beginning of each of your turns, under normal circumstances, you recover 1 SP plus 1 for every 10 full points of your maximum (e.g. if you have 6 SP you recover 1/turn; if you have 10 SP you recover 2/turn; and if you have 26 SP you recover 3/turn); however, if you have 0 AP before the start of your turn, you do not recover SP that turn.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-readied-actions\">Readied Actions<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;During your turn, you can choose to Ready an Action. When you do so, you may specify conditions that your character is preparing for, e.g. \u201cif I am attacked\u201d, and if those conditions are met you take the Action outside of your turn as though it were a Reaction, if you still have the AP and SP required to do so. You may also use your Readied Action as an ordinary Action at any point during your turn.</p>\n<p>&emsp;You must spend 1 AP to Ready an action on the turn that you do so, whether or not the conditions are met before your next turn, and in addition to the standard AP cost that the action incurs when it is taken; additionally, you must enter a state of Focus when you Ready an Action and maintain it until you take the Action; if your Focus is broken, the Readied Action is forfeit.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you wish to use a Readied Action to interrupt its triggering Action or Effect before it resolves, you may do so at the QM\u2019s discretion, but they may ask you to make a Ref Check as a Free Action to see if your character can make it in time \u2013 this may be a Contested Check against the Ref or other skill of another being taking the triggering Action, or it may be a Check against a triggering Effect with a CR determined by the QM. If you fail the check, you take the Action as a standard Reaction after the triggering Action or Effect resolves.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-attacking-and-defending\">Attacking and Defending<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;In a combat encounter in which you are being accosted or need to dispatch an enemy, the most important actions involve attacking and defending. When you make an attack action, you make one roll to hit and a second to deal damage. To hit, roll a D20 (if you\u2019re using a martial weapon and you have no training in a weapon of that size or style, make the roll with disadvantage), add your SA score, add or subtract any other modifiers that may apply to the specific attack you\u2019ve selected, then subtract your target\u2019s DA score \u2013  if the result is higher than 0 the attack lands and damage is calculated; if it is 0 or lower, the target avoids the attack or it simply misses outright and there is no need to calculate damage.</p>\n<p>&emsp;To calculate damage, use the Weapon Power (WP) of the weapon you\u2019re using (which often involves your BP score and/or dice rolls to determine the total score for each attack) and any other effects that increase attack damage, and subtract the target\u2019s AC score and any other effects that decrease damage. If the result is 1 or higher, the target\u2019s HP is reduced by that amount. If it is between 0 and -10, the target loses 1 HP. If it is -10 or less, the target does not lose HP, but is still treated for the purposes of effects as though it has been successfully hit with the attack.</p>\n<p>&emsp;When an enemy attacks you and you are aware of it and have AP to spend, you may attempt to defend yourself as a reaction. The Dodge reaction allows you to spend AP and SP to increase your DA score against this attack. If you are hit, the Perry reaction allows you to subtract your weapon\u2019s Coverage Rating (CR) roll from the final hit score, and if you have a shield the Block reaction allows you to subtract your shield\u2019s CR score and roll from the final hit score \u2013 if the result is still higher than 0, you still take damage regardless; if it is reduced to 0 or less, you are still treated as though you are hit, but you do not take damage.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-health-points-and-damage\">Health Points and Damage<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;Your Health Points (HP) is a measure of how much bodily damage you can absorb. If you take damage, such as being hit by a successful attack, your current HP is reduced by the number of damage points you take. </p>\n<p>&emsp;If you take more than 25% of your HP score in damage from one hit, you receive an Injury Token (IT) for each full 25% (e.g. for a character with an HP score of 20, taking 5 damage gives you 1 IT, taking 10 damage gives you 2 IT, and taking 15 damage gives you 3 IT; this happens regardless of your current HP level).</p>\n<p>&emsp;If your HP is reduced to 0 or below, you are Incapacitated and cannot stand on your own or take Actions. If your HP is reduced to lower than -10% of your HP score, your character dies (e.g. for a character with 20 HP, your HP being reduced to -3 means death).</p>\n<p>&emsp;HP can only be recovered by taking a Rest or being targeted by a healing Effect.</p>\n<p>&emsp;Injury Tokens and the Incapacitated condition are discussed in detail in the Conditions, Afflictions, and Bonuses subsection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-standard-encounter-actions\">Standard Encounter Actions<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;The following is a list of basic actions that any character can take during an encounter and descriptions of how to use them, their cost, and what they do. It is not comprehensive, but it forms the basics of how to conduct an encounter.</p>\n<p>Attack:</p>\n<p>&emsp;When you make an attack, there are specific attack actions you choose to use. If you are unarmed, you must use an Unarmed Attack unless you have another attack available to you (for example because of an Effect). If you have a weapon in hand, you may use a Weapon Attack, any of the attacks specified in the weapon\u2019s description, or any other attacks available to you while wielding that weapon type. If you are using a martial weapon you are not trained in, you have disadvantage on your hit roll; if you are attacking with an object that is not a weapon, it is an Improvised Weapon Attack.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Unarmed Attack</u></p>\n<p>&emsp;Cost: &emsp;&emsp;2 AP</p>\n<p>&emsp;Range:&emsp;&emsp;Melee</p>\n<p>&emsp;Hit: &emsp;&emsp;1D12+SA</p>\n<p>&emsp;Damage: &emsp;BP</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Improvised Weapon Attack</u></p>\n<p>&emsp;Cost: &emsp;&emsp;3 AP</p>\n<p>&emsp;Range:&emsp;&emsp;1, or Ath Mod (min 3) if thrown</p>\n<p>&emsp;Hit:&emsp;&emsp;1D12+SA</p>\n<p>&emsp;Damage: &emsp;BP+1D6</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Weapon Attack</u></p>\n<p>&emsp;Cost:&emsp;&emsp;2 AP</p>\n<p>&emsp;Range: &emsp;Weapon Range</p>\n<p>&emsp;Hit: &emsp;&emsp;1D12+SA</p>\n<p>&emsp;Damage:&emsp;Weapon Power (WP)</p>\n<ul><li>You can use a Weapon Attack with any simple or martial weapon you are wielding; any additional attacks you can use with the weapon are listed under its properties. </li><li>When using the Weapon Attack action, you may choose to expend additional AP and/or SP to increase your SA and or BP. For every additional AP you expend, your SA increases by 10% of your SA rounded down (min 1), and for every additional SP you expend, your BP increases by 10% of your BP rounded down (min 1)</li><li>If you are using a martial weapon and you are not trained in a weapon type with the same size and style as the martial weapon you are using, you have disadvantage to hit with it.</li></ul>\n<p>Move:</p>\n<p>&emsp;When you take the move action, your character can move in a straight line any distance up to a number of meters equal to your RD. If you move half or less of your max distance, you expend 2 AP; if you move more than half your max distance, you expend 3 AP. If you take a subsequent move action to continue in the same direction, you expend 2 AP up to your max distance. Each move action also expends 2 SP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you attack a target while moving, the AP cost of both the attack and move actions together is whichever is higher between the two. For the attack, your SA is reduced by 20% of your speed, and your BP is increased by 20% of your speed if the attack deals blunt or piercing damage. If the target blocks or parries the attack it ends the movement.</p>\n<p>Shift:</p>\n<p>&emsp;You expend 1 AP and move 1 m in a direction of your choice.</p>\n<p>Dodge:</p>\n<p>&emsp;This Action can only be taken as a Readied Action and interrupts an Attack or other damaging Effect. You take the Shift action, expending 1 AP and moving 1 m. If the move puts you outside the attack\u2019s intended area of effect (even if you are still in range), add 1D6 to your DA score for every 10 points of your total score, and if the resulting total would cause the attack to miss, it misses.</p>\n<p>Parry/Block:</p>\n<p>&emsp;This Reaction interrupts an attack while you are wielding a weapon/shield with a CR score. Expend 2 AP and add your weapon\u2019s/shield\u2019s CR score to your DA, and if the resulting total would cause the attack to miss, it does 0 damage. You are still treated as though you were successfully hit by the attack for the purpose of other effects.</p>\n<p>Push:</p>\n<p>&emsp;Make an Unarmed Attack against a target, but instead of dealing damage on hit, move the target a number of meters away from you equal to (your WC)-(target\u2019s WC)+(10% of your BP).</p>\n<p>Skill Check:</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you wish to perform a more creative Action during an Encounter (e.g. catch a thrown item, wrestle an opponent into submission, search for a target in a crowd, etc.), you must make a Skill Check to accomplish it. Making a Skill Check requires 3 AP (or more/other costs, at the QM\u2019s discretion), and if you beat the CR your QM sets for the Check the Action succeeds with no further cost required.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-combo-attacks\">Combo Attacks<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>One special kind of Attack Action all characters can make is called a Combo Attack. In order to perform a Combo Attack, you must choose another willing character and declare that you are doing a Combo Attack with them; expend 3 AP and roll a Skill Check from the Combo Check table to trigger the action. The character who triggered the action is called the Leader for the action, and the character declared as the one with which they are doing the Combo is called the Finisher.</p>\n<p>Once a Combo Attack has been triggered by the Leader, play continues as normal until the Finisher\u2019s next turn. On the Finisher\u2019s turn, if the Leader and the Finisher are both within attack range of the same target, the Finisher can initiate the Combo Attack; the Leader and the Finisher roll an attack at the same time, both expending the normal AP cost of their attack in order to do so (if either one does not have enough AP or is not in range, the Combo Attack does not initiate); look at the results of each character\u2019s roll to hit after modifiers have been added to determine what happens next.</p>\n<p>If both results are lower than the target\u2019s DA, the Combo Attack fails, but the Finisher may make a second roll to hit with their attack and take the higher result of the two; if this causes the attack to hit, it is treated as a normal attack.</p>\n<p>If the Leader\u2019s attack hits, the Finisher gains a bonus to their attack from the Combo Check table based on the results of their Skill Check to initiate the Combo Attack. If both attacks hit, both the Leader and the Finisher gain this bonus.</p>\n<p>If at least one attack hits, determine the difference between the results of the rolls to hit. If the difference is 1-3, the action is a Critical Combo; if the difference is 0, the action is a Perfect Combo.</p>\n<p>For a Critical Combo, apply the next level Bonus from the Combo Check table; if both attacks hit, add the results of both damage rolls together and treat the sum as one instance of damage against the target\u2019s AC.</p>\n<p>For a Perfect Combo, apply the max Bonus from the Combo Check table; add the results of both damage rolls together, double it, and treat the sum as one instance of damage against the target\u2019s AC.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-combo-check-table\">Combo Check Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><th></th><th>CL 10</th><th>CL 15</th><th>CL 20</th><th>CL 25</th><th>Perfect</th></tr><tr><td>Battlewise</td><td>Target loses 1 AP.</td><td>Target loses 2 AP.</td><td>Target loses 3 AP.</td><td>Target loses 4 AP.</td><td>Target loses 4 AP and skips its next turn.</td></tr><tr><td>Intimidate</td><td>Target shifts away 1 as a reaction.</td><td>Target moves away 3 as a reaction.</td><td>Target moves away RD as a reaction.</td><td>Target moves away RD as a reaction, and again as an action next turn.</td><td>Target moves away RD as a reaction and cannot attack you next turn.</td></tr><tr><td>Athletics</td><td>BP +2</td><td>BP +3</td><td>BP +4</td><td>BP +5</td><td>BP +10</td></tr><tr><td>Acrobatics</td><td>You may Shift as a free action.</td><td>You may move up to 2 as a free action.</td><td>You may move up to half your RD as a free action.</td><td>You may move up to your RD as a free action.</td><td>You may move up to your RD as a free action and regain 50% of your max SP.</td></tr><tr><td>Grappling</td><td>Target is knocked prone.</td><td>Target is knocked prone and drops whatever it is holding.</td><td>Target is knocked prone and whatever it is holding is thrown 5 m away.</td><td>Target is knocked prone and you may take an item it is holding with your free hand.</td><td>Target is knocked prone and stunned and you may take an item it is holding with your free hand.</td></tr><tr><td>Tracking</td><td>SA +3</td><td>SA +5</td><td>SA +7</td><td>SA +10</td><td>Critical hit, next hit is a critical hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Courage</td><td>Target loses 5 SP</td><td>Target loses 8 SP</td><td>Target loses 10 SP</td><td>Target loses 12 SP</td><td>Target is winded and stunned.</td></tr><tr><td>Handling</td><td>Target has -2 to its next Skill Check</td><td>Target has -4 to its next Skill Check</td><td>Target has -6 to its next Skill Check</td><td>Target has -8 to its next Skill Check</td><td>Target fails its next Skill Check</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"rules-conditions-afflictions-bonuses\">Conditions, Afflictions, &amp; Bonuses<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"rules-advantage-and-disadvantage\">Advantage and Disadvantage<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;A character can have her actions affected by certain conditions within herself and in the environment. When you are making a check circumstances can put you at either advantage or disadvantage; this can be either because of an effect or simply at the discretion of the QM. When you have advantage, roll two dice instead of one (2D12 for an Attack roll or a Block/Parry roll, 2D20 for any other roll that uses a D20), and take the more favorable of the two outcomes as the result of your roll; when at disadvantage, you do the same thing but take the less favorable of the two instead.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If one effect would cause you to have advantage and another to have disadvantage on the same roll, you roll a single die as normal. If two effects would cause you to have advantage, or two effects would cause you to have disadvantage, you have great advantage or severe disadvantage, respectively; under which you roll three dice instead of two (3D12 for an Attack roll or a Block/Parry roll, 3D20 otherwise) and take the best/worst of the three. If you have more cumulative advantage or disadvantage than that, you are simply treated as though you rolled the highest number on the die (a 12 on a D12, a 20 on a D20) or a 1, respectively.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-focus-and-concentration\">Focus and Concentration<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;When your character is Focused on a task any hands or senses required for the task are considered occupied and any Actions you take outside of the Effect requiring Focus have their AP cost doubled, you have disadvantage on Skill Checks not related to the task, and if you take damage or are otherwise moved or attacked you must make a Men Skill Check with a CR of 10+(damage taken x 2 / distance moved x 2) or the Focus ends.</p>\n<p>&emsp;When your character is Concentrating on a task you cannot do anything else including move, speak, or actively use your senses. You have severe disadvantage on Skill Checks and if you attempt to make one Concentration ends. If you take damage or are otherwise moved or attacked Concentration ends.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-status-conditions\">Status Conditions<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;Status conditions are bodily or mental effects that your character can find themselves under when specific conditions are met. For example, if a character has lost eight WP, they become affected by the status condition Sleep Deprivation, and the condition is removed when they regain enough WP or it is negated by a specific healing effect. Common status conditions are listed below, but in general any nonspecific effect on a character that is temporary and situation-dependent is likely considered a status condition.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Exhaustion:</u> Exhaustion is treated similarly to reductions in SuP, although characters will have different levels of EP depending on their Skill levels and expend them through specific activities. If your character has an EP max lower than the threshold of an exhaustion level, she takes that level upon expending 1 EP.</p>\n<ul><li>Exhaustion 1: Active at 15 EP or less. Your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 20% and you take a -2 penalty to your modifier when making a Skill Check.</li><li>Exhaustion 2: Active at 10 EP or less. Your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 40%, you take a -3 penalty to your modifier when making a Skill Check, and you have disadvantage on Skill Checks.</li><li>Exhaustion 3: Active at 5 EP or less. Your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 50%, you take a -5 penalty to your modifier when making a Skill Check, and you have disadvantage on Skill Checks and rolls to hit or defend.</li><li>Exhaustion 4: Active at 0 EP. You are rendered unconscious and sleep for a minimum of 4 hours. If you would normally lose more EP, you take damage equal to 20% of your max HP for each point.</li></ul>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Winded:</u> Active when your character is at 0 SP. When winded, you can\u2019t recover SP until the end of your third turn after the condition takes effect. For the duration taking any action requires +1 AP. Taking an action that normally requires SP is possible, but instead of reducing your SP its AP cost is doubled in addition to the baseline +1; additionally, if it would cost more than 3 SP you must make an Ath, Fit, or Cou check with a CR of ((SP Cost)x5) or the action fails.<br>&emsp;While Winded, you do not regain SP. At the end of your third consecutive turn of being Winded, the condition ends and you may regain SP at the start of your next turn. If an effect causes you to gain SP while Winded, the condition ends.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Overencumbered:</u> Active anytime you are carrying more total weight than your CC score. Your RD is halved, you cannot take the Jump or Dodge actions, you sink at a rate of 1 m /round while swimming, and long distance travel requires twice as much EP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you are carrying more total weight than double your CC score, your RD is reduced to 10%, you cannot Jump, Shift, swim, climb, or long distance travel, and you must expend 1 SP /round to remain standing and do not recover SP while standing; if you are Winded, you either drop what you are carrying or fall Prone.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Prone:</u> Your character is lying down, army crawling, or otherwise extremely low to the ground. Your RD is reduced to 20% of your RD score and you have disadvantage on all rolls to attack; rolls to hit you at range 0-2 have advantage, and rolls to hit you at range 10 or greater have disadvantage. You may end the condition as an Action by expending 3 AP.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Stunned:</u> You treated as though you are in Focus for the duration, except there is no target and it does not end except by ending the Stunned condition. At the start of your turn, you can make a Men Skill Check with a CR of 15 to end the condition (if you fail the save three times in a row, the condition ends automatically at the end of the following turn).</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Incapacitated:</u> If standing or otherwise held aloft by your own power, you fall Prone. You are treated as though you are Stunned for the duration of the effect. Your AP becomes 0 and you do not recover AP for the duration of the effect. When the duration of the condition specified in the effect ends (if none specified, it is 0), the condition does not end but beginning the following turn you may make a Recovery Skill Check with a CR of 20 at the beginning of your turn, and if successful the condition ends (if you fail the save three times in a row, the condition ends automatically at the end of the following turn).</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Bleeding</u>: At the beginning of each of your turns, as well as any time you are hit with an attack or expend SP, you gain a Bleed Token (BT). If no duration is specified in the effect that caused the condition, it ends after 3 minutes. If multiple Effects cause you to be affected by this Condition, you gain a BT for each Effect every time you would gain 1.</p>\n<p>&emsp;BT have the following properties whether or not you are affected by the Bleeding condition: For every 20 BT you have, your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 10%.; if you have 20 BT or more, the SP cost of all Actions and Reactions is doubled; if you have 50 BT or more, you are Stunned for the duration; if you have 100 BT or more, you are Incapacitated for the duration; for each BT you would gain beyond 100, you lose 1 HP instead. Taking a Respite removes 1D4 BT, a Short Rest removes 2D4 BT, a  Full Rest removes 10+2D8 BT, a Long Rest removes 20+2D12 BT, and an Extended Rest removes all BT.</p>\n<p>&emsp;<u>Wounded / Severely Wounded / Fatally Wounded:</u> When you take damage of any type other than psychic equal to or exceeding 25% of your max HP from a single Attack or Effect, you gain a Wound Token (WT); if the damage equals or exceeds 50% you gain 2 WT, and if it equals or exceeds 75% you gain 3 WT; as long as you have at least one WT, you are affected by the Wounded condition. For each WT you have, your Aggregate Stat values are reduced by 10% until it is removed. If you have 2 WT or more, you also have -5 to all modifiers for Skill Checks; if you have 3 WT or more, you also have disadvantage on all Skill Checks. One WT is removed every 5 days or each time you take an Extended Rest.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you gain 2 WT from a single Attack or Effect, you are affected by the Severely Wounded condition; your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 10% and you have -2 to modifiers for Skill Checks in addition to the WT reduction, and you must roll on the Bodily Harm table to assign an additional Effect or Condition to your character. The Severely Wounded condition is removed and becomes the Wounded condition after 3 days or when you take a Long Rest.</p>\n<p>&emsp;If you gain 3 WT from a single Attack or Effect, you are affected by the Fatally Wounded condition; your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 20% and you have -4 to modifiers for Skill Checks in addition to the WT reduction, and you must roll on the Bodily Disaster table to assign an additional Effect or Condition to your character. Additionally, you are Stunned for the duration of this effect. The Fatally Wounded condition is removed and becomes the Severely Wounded condition after 3 days or when you take a Long Rest, or it becomes the Wounded condition when you take an Extended Rest.</p>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Wounded</th><th colspan=\"2\">Severely Wounded</th><th colspan=\"2\">Fatally Wounded</th></tr><tr><td>1 WT</td><td>-10% Stats</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2 WT</td><td>-20% Stats<br>-3 Skill Checks</td><td>2 WT</td><td>-30% Stats<br>-5 Skill Checks</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3 WT</td><td>-30% Stats<br>-3 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>3 WT</td><td>-40% Stats<br>-5 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>3 WT</td><td>-50% Stats<br>-7 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td></tr><tr><td>4 WT</td><td>-40% Stats<br>-3 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>4 WT</td><td>-50% Stats<br>-5 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>4 WT</td><td>-60% Stats<br>-7 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td></tr><tr><td>5 WT</td><td>-50% Stats<br>-3 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>5 WT</td><td>-60% Stats<br>-5 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td><td>5 WT</td><td>-70% Stats<br>-7 Skill Checks<br>Disadvantage SC</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"rules-bodily-harm-table\">Bodily Harm Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;The following represents a general picture of injuries a character may sustain as a result of taking the Severely Wounded condition or other reasons as determined by the QM. Roll 1D6 and match the row of the number rolled to the column of the damage type. Feel free to adjust the flavor text and add specifics as appropriate to the situation.</p>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Blunt/Force</th><th>Sharp</th><th>Poison</th><th>Burn</th><th>Shock</th><th>Corrosive</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Fractured Leg/Foot:<br>You cannot walk or stand on the affected leg unassisted for 7 days.</td><td>Finger Loss:<br>Your QM may have you roll a D4 to decide which finger you lose, or you can pick. You are Bleeding. You cannot use the affected hand until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Sickness:<br>You spend the next two rounds retching and can take no other actions.</td><td>Blistered Leg/Foot:<br>Your RD is halved until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Overload:<br>You are incapacitated.</td><td>Weapon Damage:<br>If you are wielding a weapon, the nonmagical damage you deal with it after AC calculation is halved permanently.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Fractured Arm/Hand:<br>You cannot use the affected arm to attack, grapple, block,  or hold items for 7 days.</td><td>Facial Gash:<br>You are Bleeding for 1 minute. The wound becomes a scar after the Wounded condition is removed. Blood may cause your vision to be partially obscured.</td><td>Mind Fog:<br>You have disadvantage on checks to maintain Focus or Concentration until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Blistered Arm/Hand:<br>When using the affected arm, your BP is halved and you cannot grapple until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Awe:<br>You are Stunned for 2D6 minutes.</td><td>Armor Damage:<br>If you are wearing armor, its AC bonus is halved permanently.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Broken Rib: Your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 10% for 7 days.</td><td>Gash/Puncture:<br>You are Bleeding. The wound becomes a scar after the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Minor Shock:<br>You have disadvantage on Skill Checks for 1D6 hours.</td><td>Blistered: Your Base AC is reduced by 10% until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Jelly Limbs: Your base BP and RD are reduced by 20% for 2D4 hours.</td><td>Chem Burn:<br>Your Base AC is reduced by 10% until the Wounded condition is removed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Broken Rib: Your Aggregate Stats are reduced by 10% for 7 days.</td><td>Gash/Puncture:<br>You are Bleeding. The wound becomes a scar after the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Fatigue:<br>Your current and max EP are reduced by 3 until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Blistered: Your Base AC is reduced by 10% until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Seize-Up: You drop whatever you are holding and fall prone.</td><td>Chem Burn:<br>Your Base AC is reduced by 10% until the Wounded condition is removed.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Concussion:<br>You are Stunned for 1D4 days.</td><td>Leg Gash:<br>You are Bleeding. Your RD is halved until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Dizzy:<br>Your RD is halved and you have disadvantage on any check to avoid falling prone for 1D6 hours.</td><td>On Fire:<br>You take 1D6 burn damage at the start of each of your turns until you or an adjacent ally expend 5 AP snuffing the flame as an Action, or it is extinguished by other means. Does not activate if you are covered in non-flammable material.</td><td>Seize-Up: You drop whatever you are holding and fall prone.</td><td>Chem Burn:<br>Your Base AC is reduced by 10% until the Wounded condition is removed.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Nasty Bruise: No effect, but it is very tender and looks ugly.</td><td>Cosmetic Cuts:<br>No effect, but you appear bloodied.</td><td>Gray Face:<br>No effect, but you appear sick.</td><td>Bad Sunburn:<br>No effect, but your skin and hair are singed.</td><td>Static:<br>No effect, but your hair stands on end.</td><td>Chem Peel:<br>No effect, but your skin is sensitive yet smooth and radiant.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"rules-bodily-disaster-table\">Bodily Disaster Table<a class=\"totop\" href=\"#rules\" data-goto=\"rules-top\" aria-label=\"Back to contents\">^ CONTENTS</a></h3>\n<p>&emsp;The following represents a general picture of injuries a character may sustain as a result of taking the Fatally Wounded condition or other reasons as determined by the QM. Roll 1D6 and match the row of the number rolled to the column of the damage type. Feel free to adjust the flavor text and add specifics (or re-roll) as appropriate to the situation.</p>\n<div class=\"rtable\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Blunt/Force</th><th>Sharp</th><th>Poison</th><th>Burn</th><th>Shock</th><th>Corrosive</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Brain Damage:<br>You are Stunned for 2D6 days. Your Stability Score is reduced by 40.</td><td>Neck Wound:<br>Roll 1D10. On 3-10, your neck is cut severely and you are Bleeding x3 for 5 minutes; on 1-2, your throat/jugular is cut or you are decapitated and you die.</td><td>Systemic Shock:<br>You are Incapacitated for 1 minute. You have disadvantage on all Skill Checks until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Third Degree Burns:<br>Affects one limb or the torso. If torso, your AC is reduced by 5; If arm, you have disadvantage on rolls (including attacks) that use that arm; if leg, your base RD is halved; until the Wounded condition is removed. </td><td>Cardiac Damage:<br>You lose 1 HP each time you accumulate +5 BT or whenever you become Winded.</td><td>Destroyed Leg/Foot:<br>Your leg/foot is stripped bare or dissolved completely and can no longer be used.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Spinal Fracture:<br>You cannot use or feel your legs until the Wounded condition is removed, and your RD is halved for 15+1D10 days after that.</td><td>Punctured Lung:<br>You are bleeding x2. Your base EP is reduced by 5 and your SP is halved until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Liver Failure:<br>You take 1 poison damage every hour for the next 4D6 hours. Any poison damage you take from other attacks or effects is doubled for the duration.</td><td>Third Degree Burns:<br>Affects one limb or the torso. If torso, your AC is reduced by 5; If arm, you have disadvantage on rolls (including attacks) that use that arm; if leg, your base RD is halved; until the Wounded condition is removed. </td><td>Cardiac Damage:<br>You lose 1 HP each time you accumulate +5 BT or whenever you become Winded.</td><td>Destroyed Leg/Foot:<br>Your leg/foot is stripped bare or dissolved completely and can no longer be used.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Internal Bleeding:<br>You are Bleeding for 5 minutes. The duration of this Effect is not reduced by Salves.</td><td>Punctured Gut: <br>You are Bleeding for 5 minutes. The benefits you gain from taking a Rest are halved until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Lingering Sickness:<br>You vomit every 1D6 hours for the next 1D2 days. During this time, you recover half HuP and ThP.</td><td>Eye Loss:<br>You lose an eye. You have disadvantage on checks that require vision until the Wounded condition is removed; if you only had one eye before, you are blind.</td><td>Explosive Grounding:<br>Your hand or foot is destroyed. You are incapacitated for 3 Rounds.</td><td>Destroyed Arm/Hand:<br>Your arm/hand is stripped bare or dissolved completely and can no longer be used.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Shattered Arm/Hand:<br>You cannot use the effected arm/hand for 7+1D10 days. If it is not treated with a DC 40+ Med check within 24 hours, you have disadvantage on all checks or attack rolls using that arm/hand permanently.</td><td>Severed Hand:<br>You are Bleeding for 5 minutes. You lose the affected hand.</td><td>Lingering Sickness:<br>You vomit every 1D4 hours for the next 1D4 days. During this time, you recover half HuP and ThP.</td><td>Major Neuralgia:<br>Your AC is reduced by 5 and you have disadvantage on Athletics or Carrying checks until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Lingering Spasms:<br>Anytime you roll a 1 to attack or on a skill check to perform a physically strenuous action, you fail, drop whatever you are holding, and must make a CL 15 Reflex, Fitness, or Soundness check to avoid falling prone, until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Eye Damage:<br>You are blinded for 2D12 hours. After your vision is restored, you have -10 SA on attacks with range 5 or greater and disadvantage on all skill checks that require vision. If you are treated with a CL 40+ Med check within 48 hours this effect lasts until the Wounded condition is removed; otherwise, it is permanent.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Shattered Arm/Hand:<br>You cannot use the effected arm/hand for 7+1D10 days. If it is not treated with a DC 40+ Med check within 24 hours, you have disadvantage on all checks or attack rolls using that arm/hand permanently.</td><td>Severed Arm:<br>You are Bleeding x2 for 5 minutes. You lose the affected arm.</td><td>Lingering Fatigue:<br>Your base EP is reduced by 5 until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Disfigurement:<br>You suffer horrible scarring on a noticeable part of your body.</td><td>Lingering Spasms:<br>Anytime you roll a 1 to attack or on a  skill check to perform a physically strenuous action, you fail, drop whatever you are holding, and must make a CL 15 Reflex, Fitness, or Soundness check to avoid falling prone, until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Disfigurement:<br>You suffer horrible scarring on a noticeable part of your body.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Shattered Leg/Foot:<br>You cannot use the affected leg for 7+1D12 days. If it is not treated with a DC 40+ Med check within 24 hours, your base RD is halved permanently.</td><td>Severed Leg: <br>You are bleeding x3 for 5 minutes. You lose the affected leg.</td><td>Kidney Damage:<br>You regain half WP until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Disfigurement:<br>You suffer horrible scarring on a noticeable part of your body.</td><td>Major Neuralgia:<br>Your AC is reduced by 5 and you have disadvantage on Athletics or Carrying checks until the Wounded condition is removed.</td><td>Disfigurement:<br>You suffer horrible scarring on a noticeable part of your body.</td></tr></table></div>" };
